# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <fts.h>

typedef struct _ftsent {
  struct _ftsent *fts_cycle;
  struct _ftsent *fts_parent;
  struct _ftsent *fts_link;
  long fts_number;
  void *fts_pointer;
  char *fts_accpath;
  char *fts_path;
  int fts_errno;
  int fts_symfd;
  unsigned short fts_pathlen;
  unsigned short fts_namelen;
  ino_t fts_ino;
  dev_t fts_dev;
  nlink_t fts_nlink;
  short fts_level;
  unsigned short fts_info;
  unsigned short fts_flags;
  unsigned short fts_instr;
  struct stat *fts_statp;
  char fts_name[1];
} FTSENT;

typedef struct {
  FTSENT *fts_cur;
  int fts_options;
  void *__td_walk;
} FTS;

FTS *fts_open(char *const *paths, int options,
              int (*compar)(const FTSENT **, const FTSENT **));
FTSENT *fts_read(FTS *fts);
int fts_set(FTS *fts, FTSENT *entry, int instruction);
int fts_close(FTS *fts);
```

# DESCRIPTION

These are BSD's `fts` calls, with glibc's constants and field names; the layout is the library's own.

`fts_open` starts a walk of the null-terminated array <paths>.
If <compar> is not null the roots, and the entries of each directory, are sorted by it; otherwise the roots are in the order given and a directory's entries in the order it lists them.
`FTS_NOSTAT` in <options> leaves entries other than the roots and directories unstat'd, as `FTS_NSOK`.

`fts_read` returns the next entry of the walk, depth first, or a null pointer at the end with `errno` set to 0.
Each directory is returned twice: before its entries (`FTS_D`) and after them (`FTS_DP`).
Files are `FTS_F`.
A path that cannot be stat'd is `FTS_NS`, with the error in `fts_errno`; a directory that cannot be listed is `FTS_DNR` and has no `FTS_DP`.
The entry's `fts_path` is the root as given, or the path through it; `fts_name` its last name; `fts_level` 0 for a root, then 1 and so on; `fts_statp` the status as stat(2) gives it; `fts_ino`, `fts_nlink` and `fts_accpath` from it, `fts_dev` is always 1.
A directory's entries get their status with its listing, when the walk reads the directory, as BSD's do, not each one when it is returned; an entry for a mount point is stat'd by its path.
An entry stays valid until `fts_close`.

`fts_set` with `FTS_SKIP` on the directory last returned leaves its entries out; its `FTS_DP` follows.
Other <instruction> values are stored in `fts_instr` and do nothing.

`fts_close` frees the walk and every entry it returned, and returns 0.

